#!/bin/sh
#
# The schema half of a deploy: check the secrets, move the control plane, then
# move every customer (XIV-61, docs/architecture.md §4.2).
#
#   docker run --rm --env-file <the deployment's> <the new image> bin/deploy
#
# **Run it once per release, out of the image being released**, before the
# serving containers are replaced. It ships inside that image on purpose — see
# "Why this is a file in the repository" below — so the step can never be a
# version behind the migrations it is running.
#
# This is not a deploy. XIV-61 also asks for a deploy *definition* — which tool,
# which host, which registry, how a rollback works — and none of that is here,
# because none of it can be proved on a machine with no target to deploy to, and
# a deploy configuration that is green in CI and unproven where it matters reads
# as done while being worth less than nothing. What is here is the part a deploy
# has to do whichever tool eventually wins, and it is the part that was missing:
# **nothing called `tenant:migrate` anywhere.**
#
#
# ## The thing this fixes
#
# There are two sets of migrations and only one of them was ever run.
#
# `migrations/control` is the control plane's, one database, and the container
# entrypoint applies it on start. `migrations/tenant` is every customer's, one
# database each, and it is applied by `tenant:migrate` — which nothing invoked.
# Not the entrypoint, not a script, not a cron. So shipping an entity change
# meant new code serving every customer against the old schema, indefinitely, and
# the first sign of it was a query failing in production.
#
#
# ## Why the tenant migrations are here rather than in the entrypoint
#
# Putting them beside the control-plane migration in `frankenphp/docker-entrypoint.sh`
# is the obvious move and the wrong one, for three reasons that compound. The
# entrypoint is worth reading for the same argument stated from that end.
#
#   1. **The entrypoint runs on every container start, which is not once per
#      deploy.** A restart after an OOM kill, a health-check flap, a node
#      draining, somebody typing `docker compose restart` — all of them would
#      walk the whole registry. That is not merely wasteful: it means a routine
#      restart of one container becomes an operation over fifty customers'
#      databases, taken at whatever moment the restart happened, which is
#      precisely the kind of surprise nobody has budgeted a maintenance window
#      for.
#
#   2. **It is proportional to the customer count, in the startup path.** The
#      control-plane migration is one database, one transaction, and the
#      container cannot serve anything without it. The tenant loop is N
#      connections, N metadata reads and N migration runs, and the container is
#      not serving while it happens. At fifty customers a restart stops being a
#      restart and becomes an outage whose length is a business metric.
#
#   3. **Concurrent starts would race.** Each tenant database tracks its own
#      versions, so two containers starting at once would both compute the same
#      plan for the same fifty databases and both start applying it. Doctrine's
#      `all_or_nothing` protects one run from itself, not two runs from each
#      other. The topology is a single instance today (§4), which makes this the
#      cheapest of the three to dismiss and the most expensive to have dismissed
#      wrongly later.
#
# A one-shot step has none of those properties: it runs once, it is allowed to
# take as long as there are customers, and it is a single process by
# construction. The cost is that it has to be *called* — an entrypoint cannot be
# forgotten and this can — and that cost is paid where it belongs, in the deploy
# definition XIV-61 still has open, rather than by making every container start
# do something it has no business doing.
#
# The entrypoint keeps its control-plane migration. It is idempotent and a no-op
# once this script has run, so running it in both places costs a version query
# and buys the property that a container never serves against a control-plane
# schema older than itself.
#
#
# ## Why this is a file in the repository rather than lines in a runbook
#
# Two properties, neither of which a runbook has.
#
# **It ships with the image it deploys.** `bin/` is copied into the production
# stage, so the sequence being run is the sequence that release was written
# against. A runbook lives somewhere else and is edited by somebody who is not
# looking at this branch, which is how a deploy ends up running last month's
# steps against this month's migrations.
#
# **It cannot be half-run.** The ordering below is not decoration: the tenant
# loop reads the registry, so a release that adds a control-plane column the
# registry query needs must move the control plane first. Written down as three
# commands for a person to type, that ordering is a convention. Written here it
# is a property, and `set -e` means step two failing never reaches step three.
#
#
# ## Additive-only, and what that means for when this runs
#
# N tenant databases do not migrate atomically, and the deployment does not stop
# while they are walked. So during this script the new schema is arriving,
# customer by customer, underneath the **old** code — and after it, the new code
# arrives on top of a schema that has already moved.
#
# §4.2 of the brief decides that window rather than eliminating it: tenant
# migrations are **additive only**, expand now and contract in a later release,
# and `tests/Unit/TenantMigrationsAreAdditiveTest.php` refuses the ones that are
# not. That is what makes the ordering here safe, and it is why this runs
# *before* the containers are replaced rather than after: old code against a
# schema that has only gained things is fine, while new code against a schema
# that has not moved yet is not.

set -e

cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

if [ -t 1 ]; then
	step() { printf '\n\033[1;34m==> %s\033[0m\n' "$1"; }
else
	step() { printf '\n==> %s\n' "$1"; }
fi

# **First, and before anything touches a database.**
#
# The check itself is `App\Deployment\PlaceholderSecretGuard`, and the container
# entrypoint runs it too — that is where the guarantee lives, because a container
# that refuses to start cannot be bypassed by not running a script. It is
# repeated here because this is the earliest moment a deploy can find out, and
# the difference matters: failing now costs a one-shot container, while failing
# at the entrypoint costs whatever the orchestrator does with a service that will
# not come up.
#
# It stands down entirely outside `APP_ENV=prod`, so running this script against
# a development stack does not trip over the placeholders development is
# supposed to have.
step 'Secrets are not the ones committed in this repository'
php bin/console deploy:check-secrets

# **The control plane, one database, all or nothing.**
#
# Same invocation the entrypoint makes, deliberately: `doctrine_migrations.yaml`
# binds the bundle to the `control` entity manager, so there is no `--em` to get
# wrong and no second definition of what "the control-plane migrations" are.
step 'Control-plane schema'
php bin/console doctrine:migrations:migrate --no-interaction --all-or-nothing

# **Then every customer, and the exit code is the point.**
#
# `tenant:migrate` catches per tenant and carries on, because one unreachable
# database must not cost the other forty-nine theirs. What it does *not* do any
# more is report that as success: it exits 3 when a tenant failed, which `set -e`
# turns into this script exiting 3, which is what stops a deploy from swapping
# containers over a registry that is only mostly migrated. See the command's own
# docblock for the three codes and why 3 rather than 2.
#
# An empty registry exits 1 and stops the deploy too, which is the right answer
# for a real deployment — an installation with no customers has either not been
# provisioned yet or has lost its registry, and both are worth a human looking.
step 'Every tenant schema'
php bin/console tenant:migrate

printf '\nSchema is deployed. Replace the serving containers now.\n'
